ut of frame. The leaderboard shows the top four runners.\n\n---\n\n### **Notable Incidents**\n\n- **No athletes are seen dropping items, changing positions abruptly, or engaging in unusual behavior** in these frames.\n- **No athlete is seen stopping or giving up**. All athletes appear to be actively competing, maintaining pace, and focused on the race.\n- The only notable "incident" is the **post-race celebration** in Frame 1 and 2, where Mo Farah is seen celebrating with the flag and hugging someone, indicating his victory.\n\n---\n\n### **Summary**\n\nThe frames capture key moments of the 10,000m final, including **race progression, athlete positioning, and post-race celebration**. On-screen graphics provide detailed information on **distance, time, and rankings**. There are **no unusual incidents or athletes dropping out**; all runners are actively competing. The video highlights the **intensity and strategy** of the race, as well as the **emotional aftermath** of Mo Farah’s victory.\n'], 'condensed_response': '**Condensed Scene Summary (Frames\u202f0700\u202f–\u202f0860)**  \n\n| Frame | Time‑code (approx.) | Characters / Focus | Key Action & Visuals |\n|-------|--------------------|--------------------|----------------------|\n| **0700** |\u202fPost‑race\u202f(~00:00) | **Mo\u202fFarah (GBR)** – wrapped in a Union‑Jack flag, surrounded by photographers and officials. | Celebratory walk; no racing activity. |\n| **0720** |\u202fPost‑race\u202f(~00:02) | **Mo\u202fFarah** + an unknown companion (coach/family). | Emotional hug over a low barrier; still in the celebration zone. |\n| **0740** |\u202fEarly race\u202f(~00:10) | Whole field (mainly Ethiopian/Kenyan runners). | Wide‑shot of the pack, digital timer “1:04.9” in the corner; “London\u202f2012” logo visible. |\n| **0746** |\u202fMid‑race\u202f(~00:15) | **Kenenisa\u202fBekele (ETH)** leading, **Ibrahim\u202fJeilan (ETH)** beside him. | Lap counter: “19\u202fLAPS\u202fTO\u202fGO”, distance “2400\u202fm”, cumu